.Dd September 6, 2026
.Dt WARDEN 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm warden
.Nd namespace (jail) broker
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon owns construction of jails
.Pq Xr jail_set 2 ,
a duty removed from
.Xr init 8
and the capability
.Xr authority 8 .
It is a socket-free capability-plane service: it exposes the well-known name
.Dq Li system.Namespace
and serves each client on its own
.Xr mac_capability 4
channel.
The discovery
.Em domain
layer resolves
.Dq Li system.Namespace
only for SYSTEM-domain clients.
Jailing is
.Em consumer self-service :
a program confines itself at runtime by calling
.Xr service_enter_namespace 3 ,
whose library resolves
.Nm
and drives it.
.Nm
is never driven by
.Xr serviced 8
on a unit's behalf, and a unit does not declare a jail in its manifest.
.Pp
.Nm
holds no standing jail authority of its own, and self-jailing needs no
per-caller token: because confining oneself is self-confinement,
.Nm
scopes each jail by the caller's unforgeable channel label, so one consumer
can never name or reuse another's jail.
.Nm
creates the jail rooted at the requested path and returns a
.Em non-owning
descriptor as the reply's single
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
fd.
The credential stored in that descriptor
.Pq root, from Nm
is what authorizes the non-root consumer's own
.Xr jail_attach_jd 2 ,
so the consumer attaches itself before exec.
Closing the non-owning descriptor never removes the jail.
.Pp
.Nm
is launched and supervised by
.Xr serviced 8
from the
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Namespace.cap
bundle; it is not started by
.Xr init 8 ,
and it is not intended to be run from the command line.
.Ss Operations
Each request is scoped to the ONE jail the caller's unforgeable channel label
names, so no operation takes a jail name or id as a wire argument.
.Bl -tag -width ".Sy DESTROY_JAIL"
.It Sy ENTER_JAIL
Create
.Pq or reuse
the caller's jail rooted at the requested path and return its non-owning
descriptor
.Pq see above .
The request carries the jail root path
.Pq required, absolute
and optionally a hostname, an IPv4 address
.Pq Va ip4_addr ,
and an IPv6 address
.Pq Va ip6_addr ;
an empty address field means no address of that family.
The hostname defaults to the derived jail name.
.Pp
If a matching jail already exists
.Pq a persistent execution container reused across relaunches
its descriptor is returned unchanged.
Reuse is safe only when the
.Em entire
requested definition matches the existing jail: path, hostname, IPv4 address,
IPv6 address, and vnet setting.
A jail's definition is immutable, so any mismatch is a hard
.Er EEXIST
rather than a silent reattach into a differently shaped jail.
.It Sy DESTROY_JAIL
Remove the caller's jail
.Pq Xr jail_remove 2 .
It replies
.Er ENOENT
when the caller has no jail, and status 0 once the jail is gone.
An ephemeral jail whose lifetime a worker anchors is torn down regardless.
.It Sy LIST_JAILS
Report the caller's jail definition: exactly zero or one jail, since a label
owns at most one.
When present it reports the jail id, root path, hostname, IPv4 and IPv6
addresses, and the
.Sy WARDEN_F_*
flags that describe it, including
.Sy WARDEN_F_VNET
.Pq the jail has its own virtual network stack
and whether the jail is ephemeral
.Pq its lifetime anchored by this connection's worker
versus persistent.
.El
.Ss Jail lifetime
Without
.Sy WARDEN_F_EPHEMERAL
a jail is
.Em persistent :
created
.Li persist=1 ,
reused by label across consumer restarts, and outliving any single consumer.
With
.Sy WARDEN_F_EPHEMERAL
the jail's lifetime is bound to the consumer:
.Nm
retains a
.Em separate
owning descriptor
.Pq Dv JAIL_OWN_DESC
in the per-client worker process, hands the consumer only the non-owning
descriptor for its attach, and tears the jail down when the consumer
disconnects and the worker exits.
.Ss Virtual networking
With
.Sy WARDEN_F_VNET
the jail gets its own virtual network stack
.Pq Li vnet=new :
an independent set of interfaces, addresses, and routing rather than a
restricted view onto the host stack.
It requires a
.Sy VIMAGE
kernel and is part of the jail's immutable definition.
An
.Va ip4_addr
or
.Va ip6_addr
without this flag is instead the jail's address on the host stack.
.Ss Operation
.Nm
runs as root and, unlike most capability-plane providers, NOT in capability
mode:
.Xr jail_set 2
needs the classic
.Dv PRIV_JAIL_SET
privilege and resolves the jail root path against the global namespace, both of
which capsicum forbids.
Its authority is the caller's channel label, not the capsicum sandbox.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /Capabilities/System/Namespace.cap" -compact
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/Namespace.cap
capability bundle shipping
.Nm
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jail_attach_jd 2 ,
.Xr jail_remove 2 ,
.Xr jail_set 2 ,
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr service_enter_namespace 3 ,
.Xr mac_capability 4 ,
.Xr authority 8 ,
.Xr jail 8 ,
.Xr serviced 8 ,
.Xr sysextd 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
daemon appeared in
.Fx 5BSD .
It takes over jail construction formerly performed inside the capability
authority
.Pq Xr authority 8
as part of moving privileged work out of PID 1 and behind named,
domain-restricted, socket-free services.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kory Heard
